=== DIAGNOSIS ===
is_anomaly: true
faulty_devices: router4
diagnosis_text:
**Affected component:** FRR RIP daemon configuration on router4 — the routing protocol configuration
has been completely wiped. The FRR running-config on router4 contains nothing except the bare
hostname line; no `router rip` block, no network statements, no routing policy of any kind.

**Mechanism:** All neighboring routers in this topology (router3 and router5) run RIPv2. Router3
advertises and learns routes over 10.0.0.0/8, 172.25.0.0/16, and 192.168.100.0/24. Router5
advertises and learns over 172.20.0.0/16 and 192.168.15.0/24. Because router4 has no RIP
instance, it never sends RIP updates to either neighbor, so neither router3 nor router5 ever
installs a route for 192.168.200.0/24 (Location C's LAN). Inbound traffic from any remote site
destined for pc11–pc14 is black-holed at the upstream routers. Simultaneously, router4 only has
a kernel default route (via 172.25.1.2/router3) and no learned RIP routes, so return traffic
cannot be correctly forwarded even for sessions initiated locally.

**Localization:** The fault is entirely on router4. Its eth0 (192.168.200.1/24, toward switch4)
and upstream links (eth4 192.168.15.2 to router5, eth5 172.25.1.1 to router3) are all physically
UP and addressed correctly — the L1/L2 and IP layers are fine. The sole failure is the missing
`router rip` block in FRR.

**Root cause:** Router4's entire FRR RIP configuration has been erased, causing it to neither
advertise Location C's 192.168.200.0/24 subnet nor learn remote routes from its RIP-speaking
neighbors.

**Corrective fix:** On router4, enter vtysh and configure:
  router rip
   network 192.168.200.0/24
   network 192.168.15.0/24
   network 172.25.0.0/16
   version 2
  exit
This will immediately re-advertise 192.168.200.0/24 into the RIP domain and restore full
bidirectional reachability for pc11–pc14 to all other sites.
=== END ===
```